1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,627
<font color="#000000">What if a robot were told what to do by analogy,
not by an exact goal image?</font>

2
00:00:05,707 --> 00:00:11,588
<font color="#000000">This ICML 2022 work from Berkeley and Meta
introduces goal-conditioned bisimulation: an</font>

3
00:00:11,668 --> 00:00:17,206
<font color="#000000">abstraction where behaviorally equivalent
state-goal pairs share a representation,</font>

4
00:00:17,286 --> 00:00:19,330
<font color="#000000">so goals compose by arithmetic.</font>

5
00:00:19,710 --> 00:00:25,174
<font color="#000000">Goal-conditioned reinforcement learning usually
hands the agent the exact goal,</font>

6
00:00:25,254 --> 00:00:26,437
<font color="#000000">as a target image.</font>

7
00:00:26,517 --> 00:00:31,069
<font color="#000000">But in the real world you rarely know the precise
goal in advance.</font>

8
00:00:31,149 --> 00:00:36,684
<font color="#000000">Ask a robot to close a drawer, and you may not know
how the closed drawer looks.</font>

9
00:00:36,764 --> 00:00:39,210
<font color="#000000">We want to specify tasks by analogy.</font>

10
00:00:39,590 --> 00:00:42,388
<font color="#000000">Consider dicing a carrot versus a radish.</font>

11
00:00:42,468 --> 00:00:48,075
<font color="#000000">The objects differ, but the skill and the change
from start to goal are the same.</font>

12
00:00:48,155 --> 00:00:54,253
<font color="#000000">Good representations are invariant to irrelevant
details and equivariant to what varies.</font>

13
00:00:54,333 --> 00:01:00,220
<font color="#000000">Prior bisimulation handled distractors for one
task; we lift it to families of tasks.</font>

14
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,982
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

15
00:01:03,062 --> 00:01:05,786
<font color="#000000">It defines goal-conditioned bisimulation,</font>

16
00:01:05,866 --> 00:01:09,273
<font color="#000000">grouping state-goal pairs that behave equivalently.</font>

17
00:01:09,353 --> 00:01:14,676
<font color="#000000">It gives a metric-learning objective so new goals
arise from latent arithmetic.</font>

18
00:01:14,756 --> 00:01:20,010
<font color="#000000">And it proves the representation is sufficient for
any state-only reward task.</font>

19
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:22,668
<font color="#000000">GCB learns two encoders together.</font>

20
00:01:22,748 --> 00:01:29,100
<font color="#000000">Phi encodes a state-goal pair so its L1 distance
matches an on-policy bisimulation metric,</font>

21
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:32,173
<font color="#000000">capturing how differently two tasks behave.</font>

22
00:01:32,253 --> 00:01:37,818
<font color="#000000">Psi encodes a state so the goal embedding minus the
state embedding equals phi.</font>

23
00:01:37,898 --> 00:01:42,463
<font color="#000000">That makes goals composable: add an analogous pair
to your state,</font>

24
00:01:42,543 --> 00:01:45,607
<font color="#000000">then take the nearest neighbor in psi space.</font>

25
00:01:45,687 --> 00:01:48,680
<font color="#000000">Training is offline on Implicit Q-Learning.</font>

26
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:56,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">GCB is tested in a PyBullet manipulation suite of
random workspaces built from eighty-four object geometries.</font>

27
00:01:57,072 --> 00:02:00,667
<font color="#FFFFFF">Tasks span Drawer, Button-and-Drawer, and Analogy,</font>

28
00:02:00,747 --> 00:02:05,298
<font color="#FFFFFF">each also run with added video distractors to probe
robustness.</font>

29
00:02:05,378 --> 00:02:07,650
<font color="#FFFFFF">Results average over five seeds.</font>

30
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:13,097
<font color="#000000">Across six settings, GCB is top on five, and wins
on every distractor task.</font>

31
00:02:13,177 --> 00:02:19,891
<font color="#000000">It reaches about forty-five percent on Drawer with
distractors and thirty-two on Button-and-Drawer.</font>

32
00:02:19,971 --> 00:02:24,078
<font color="#000000">On analogy tasks, where the goal is inferred from
an example,</font>

33
00:02:24,158 --> 00:02:26,960
<font color="#000000">it roughly doubles the next-best baseline.</font>

34
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:29,531
<font color="#000000">Ablations confirm the design.</font>

35
00:02:29,611 --> 00:02:33,526
<font color="#000000">Adding a grounding term, phi of a goal with itself,</font>

36
00:02:33,606 --> 00:02:37,521
<font color="#000000">as a normalizing constant in psi's objective helps.</font>

37
00:02:37,601 --> 00:02:40,262
<font color="#000000">An L1 metric loss for phi beats L2.</font>

38
00:02:40,342 --> 00:02:45,040
<font color="#000000">And varying the latent dimensionality barely
changes control.</font>

39
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:48,692
<font color="#000000">Some headline numbers.</font>

40
00:02:48,772 --> 00:02:52,043
<font color="#000000">On Drawer with video distractors, GCB reaches zero
point four four eight,</font>

41
00:02:52,123 --> 00:02:55,395
<font color="#000000">best of any method.</font>

42
00:02:55,475 --> 00:02:58,747
<font color="#000000">On Button-and-Drawer with distractors, zero point
three two two.</font>

43
00:02:58,827 --> 00:03:02,098
<font color="#000000">On analogy,</font>

44
00:03:02,178 --> 00:03:05,450
<font color="#000000">zero point four zero three versus zero point one
seven six for the next best.</font>

45
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:11,397
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: treating bisimulation as an
equivalence over tasks, not just states,</font>

46
00:03:11,477 --> 00:03:13,876
<font color="#000000">aligns analogous tasks in one space.</font>

47
00:03:13,956 --> 00:03:17,044
<font color="#000000">Because goals become composable by arithmetic,</font>

48
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:21,658
<font color="#000000">you can point a robot at an example and have it
infer its own goal,</font>

49
00:03:21,738 --> 00:03:25,170
<font color="#000000">provably sufficient for any state-only reward task.</font>
